WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.930
Software has never shipped faster.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:08.250
This is OnToGraPh, and the next five minutes are
about what speed leaves behind.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.190
Scan to follow along.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.070
You describe an app. Seconds later, it runs.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.990
The tests are green, and everyone moves on.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.190
But nobody asked the one question code cannot
answer: what do these words mean?

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.730
Take a library. Two copies of a book share one
ISBN.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:37.190
A member borrows one, the app marks the title as
borrowed, and both copies disappear.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.370
Nothing crashed. The app never knew a book from
a copy.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.810
Or take one project code. Finance reads it as a
ledger entry.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.760
The project office reads it as a deliverable.
Both are right.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.150
The application picks one meaning, and never
tells you which.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.370
And this is not only vibe coding.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:05.110
Carefully engineered systems carry the same gap,
because the meaning was never written down.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.230
Add AI agents, and the confusion repeats at
machine speed.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:17.250
So we did the groundwork. A dictionary, so every
term has one definition.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.070
A taxonomy, so every term has a class.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.890
An ontology, so every relationship has a type.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.030
Three layers, one model: written once, reviewed
by people, readable by agents.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.110
That makes the model ready for agentic AI.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:43.380
An agent no longer guesses from rows. It asks
the graph, follows typed relationships,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.210
and returns an answer you can trace to a
definition a person approved.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.890
This is OnToGraPh. Ontology-driven governance
for AI and agents.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.110
Govern the rules. Verify the decisions.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.590
Four letters, four commitments.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.410
O: an operational ontology, where definitions
link to processes, controls and applications.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.930
T: traceability to every field.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.270
G: governance with human authority; people
approve, monitor, and can withdraw.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:26.910
P: proof of behavior; evidence of what each
application actually did.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.650
Here is why that last letter matters.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.110
In Aurora 21, a synthetic case, one rule feeds
two applications.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.130
Both report the same release. The CRM applies
the new rule.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.910
The PMS returns the old decision, behind a
matching receipt.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.210
A matching receipt is not proof.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.950
Withdrawal works the same way.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.330
If an application is unreachable, it stays
unverified. Never assumed.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.650
Only a fresh observation proves the rule has
stopped.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.810
So where does it sit in the market?

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.090
Catalogs, ontology editors and graph engines do
valuable work:

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.490
they verify the model and the platform.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.610
OnToGraPh verifies behavior after release; what
each application actually decided.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.130
Keep your platform, and add the layer that
proves it.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.870
Nothing is replaced. Your catalog, your graph
engine and your pipelines stay.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.710
OnToGraPh speaks open formats: JSON, JSON-LD,
SKOS, OWL and PROV-O.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.550
And it compiles reviewed rules to Rego.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.870
And you can use it today. Clone the repository,
run the demo, start the Studio.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.210
Four commands. Python and SQLite, nothing else.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.210
It runs offline, on your machine, and reproduces
Aurora 21 in front of you.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.220
Or start in the browser. Describe your own app
in a sentence,

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:12.130
and get four proposals: risks, dictionary,
taxonomy and ontology.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.370
A private draft, ready to review in Studio.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.890
A quick tour. The Process Modeler: draw the
process on a canvas,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.250
exchange inventories with Excel, and edit
properties in place.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.170
Semantic Studio: the graph itself.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.550
Switch layers, select any object, and edit every
property, visually or in JSON.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.550
Then compare drafts before release.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.910
The Evidence Workbench: delivery, receipt and
observation, side by side, for every
application.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.110
When they disagree, it says so, by name.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.970
OnToGraPh grew out of PALO Framework and
PolicyWatcher.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.630
It is their shared engine, and it stands on its
own.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.390
OnToGraPh. Govern the rules. Verify the
decisions.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.980
Scan a code, clone the repository, and bring
your own app.
